<article class="env-proposition" id="FE1A">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/FE1A">Proposition <span data-tag="FE1A">6.5.1.19</span>.</a> Let $\calX $ be an $\infty $-topos containing an object $X$, and let $\sigma : \Delta ^2 \rightarrow \calX $ be a $2$-simplex corresponding to a diagram </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix { Y \ar [dr] \ar [rr]^{f} &  &  Z \ar [dl]^{g} \\ &  X. &  } \]
</div>
  <p> Then $f$ is an $n$-connective morphism in $\calX $ if and only if $\sigma $ is an $n$-connective morphism in $\calX _{/X}$. </p>
</article>
<article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      We observe that $\calX _{/g} \rightarrow \calX _{/Z}$ is a trivial fibration, so that an object of $\calX _{/g}$ is $n$-connective if and only if its image in $\calX _{/Z}$ is $n$-connective. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>